p2. order dependence in a composite crossing is confined to the lossy region
==============================================================================

CONTROL. `71`'s narrowing case, reproduced: wide wrap -> narrow saturate.

  distinct functions        2
  agreeing source values    30/256    (`71`: 30/256)
  control reproduces        True
  first three witnesses     [(-128, 0, -8), (-127, 1, -8), (-126, 2, -8)]

H-a. WIDENING instead of narrowing: narrow wrap -> wide saturate.
     Every source value is already in the target's set, so the Q-move is exact.

  distinct functions        1
  agreeing source values    16/16
  H-a holds                 True

H-b. NARROWING, restricted to source values already inside the target's set.

  source values in range    16/256
  distinct functions        1
  agreeing source values    16/16
  H-b holds                 True
  complement (out of range) 240 values, distinct functions 2, agreeing 14/240

THE PARTITION, stated as a biconditional and checked value by value.

  values where the two orders diverge   226
  values outside the target's set       240
  the two sets are equal                False
  divergent is contained in lossy       True

  H-c AS A BICONDITIONAL IS REFUTED BY THIS PROBE'S OWN OUTPUT, and the prediction is
  kept with the correction. Containment holds in one direction only: every divergent
  value is lossy, and some lossy values agree anyway.
    lossy but agreeing: 14 values, [-120, -104, -88, -72, -56, -40, -24, 23, 39, 55, 71, 87, 103, 119]

  The closed form for the exceptions, derived after seeing them and then checked here.
  Order one is wrap onto the target; order two is clamp onto the target. Off range they
  agree exactly when the wrapped representative lands ON the bound the clamp would pick:
  above the window when v is congruent to hi, below it when v is congruent to lo.
    predicted agreeing-but-lossy set: 14 values, [-120, -104, -88, -72, -56, -40, -24, 23, 39, 55, 71, 87, 103, 119]
    predictor is exact:               True

BOTH DIRECTIONS of the reduction pair, so the finding is not about saturation.

  wrap      -> saturate   distinct 2  agreeing 30/256
  saturate  -> wrap       distinct 2  agreeing 30/256
  wrap      -> wrap       distinct 1  agreeing 256/256  (single coordinate, no order to name)
  saturate  -> saturate   distinct 1  agreeing 256/256  (single coordinate, no order to name)

==============================================================================
VERDICT

  `71` section 4's finding stands: a composite crossing over {Q, rho} is not determined
  by its endpoints, and the canon must name an order.

  Its SCOPE is narrower than the section states, in one direction and not two. The two
  orders are the same function on every source value the crossing carries WITHOUT LOSS,
  measured here at 16 of 16 in range and 16 of 16 under widening. Off range they usually
  differ and sometimes coincide, so the divergent set is contained in the lossy set and
  is not equal to it.

  The containment is the direction that matters for what op is asked. The obligation is
  not 'name an order for every multi-coordinate crossing'. It is 'name an order for every
  LOSSY one', and a crossing that loses nothing is endpoint-determined and needs no canon
  sentence at all.

  That matters because widening composites are the common case in the material this
  panel already carries: an accumulator entry, a promotion into a wider intermediate,
  and the window mechanism a chain uses are all Q-growing.

  What this does not establish: anything about three or more coordinates (`71` measured
  that and found two classes, which this file does not re-derive); anything about a
  crossing whose ambient domain also moves; any magnitude. One model pair of widths,
  exhaustive within it.
